e daughters of the  american revolution uh the american  legion etc they had very tight  relationships with the fbi they're sort  of back and forth relationships hoover  legitimated a lot of their interests  often helped these organizations out and  when he was in trouble they often came  to put pressure on congressmen and to  write letters to newspapers in defense  of j edgar hoover so i think we have to  think about him  as a political organizer as well my last  thing that i will say about j edgar  hoover as a bureaucrat is that i have  become convinced that the single most  important thing that he did in his  entire career in order to maintain power  was not in fact you know knowing about  john f kennedy's affairs or following  eleanor roosevelt and going to franklin  and reporting about who she uh in fact  was seeing the single most important  thing he did to maine power was to keep  his agents out of the civil service  system  he fought his entire career to keep his  agents out of civil service and what  this meant was that he was actually able  to build an agency in which he himself  had almost sole control over  the hiring the firing of agents the kind  of person he wanted  he justified this not on the grounds of  personal control but on the grounds of  saying that the fbi is so fabulous and  its standards are so high that they  simply cannot take the sort of mass of  people who are being recommended out of  the civil service but again these kinds  of bureaucratic questions these kinds of  state building questions i think are as  important for understanding j edgar  hoover  as um  what files he might have maintained so  that's sort of the first piece i said so  the driving force at least in part of  this biography is how does an  ideological conservative maintain state  power  throughout the age of american  liberalism so we've got a little bit of  the power story there i want to quickly  talk about the literature on  conservatism and just suggest a few ways 