ween liberalism and conservatism  because the fact of j edgar hoover and  the kinds of politics that he had  actually meant that people on the ground  activists on the ground had very very  different relationships  to state power  it is fundamentally different experience  to be organizing on the left  and we all probably basically know the  story of the fbi's relationship with the  new left with the black panthers with  the anti-war movement right it's a  fundamentally different experience to  fear that your organization is being  infiltrated uh to  know that the federal government is  watching you and is against you uh than  it is to be organizing even uh on in a  similar grassroots organization  on the right and know that in some sense  the fbi is behind you that j edgar  hoover is basically tolerant or  supportive of your politics now i don't  think this explains  everything certainly about what's going  on in social movements but to the degree  that we are talking in the 1960s now  about a kind of battle that's emerged  between the left and the right during  those years i think it's important to  see that their relationships with the  state and with the fbi in particular are  not equivalent and that they're very  different on the ground experiences in  part  because of this man who is there  at the top  in the fbi  all right just to finish up i want to  say a few things going back to our our  nixon at jagger hoover's funeral i said  you know the things that stood out to me  about that clip were number one  the fact of the funeral right the fact  that this unelected bureaucrat  was able to  bring together the kind of power and  interest and cultural influence that  hoover did in 1972 and in the years  leading up to that um the second was  that he stood for something right he  stood for a particular kind of  conservative politics and really was a  bulwark of that kind of conservative  politics throughout this period we  describe as the age of american  liberalism 