unism and  homosexuality fit in uh in a piece for  these men i think is a very interesting  question what kind of world are they  themselves living in and we have little  snippets of information i mean one of  the things that's actually particularly  interesting about hoover's relationship  with clyde tolson is that in some sense  it was extremely public right we have  this idea that they were these closeted  men and on the one hand they were they  would have denied to the ends of the  earth  that they were homosexual at least they  would have denied that publicly on the  other hand they spent 40 years together  traveling together having dinner to dig  together every night kind of going to  each other's family funerals being  really a very functional and  well-recognized social couple in  washington so i think there's a lot of  stuff that's going on in this moment and  i think uh there are two things i mean  one i'm not sure that pure  just a story of hypocrisy really gets to  the root of this i think there are  probably  more interesting things to do with it  and i think that we also have to try to  kind of historicize these questions and  things look very different in the 1930s  than they look in the 1950s around those  kinds of questions and they look  different  again in the 1970s though we can as you  noted see these continuities of of type  over time  have you consciously avoided the  language of psychology or psychology i  would have called him deeply paranoid  yes so this is this is one of the great  biographical questions right and the  question of psychobiography and where to  go i mean hoover is a very very  interesting figure on this account i  mean many people are so first of all  there's a lot of real diagnosed mental  illness in his family  his father actually dies of you know  it's a little hard to tell but but  depression he's he's institutionalized  when hoover is a teenager and dies of  some combination of depression maybe  maybe alcoholism but hi